    <h2>Phase 2: Ideation, Testing &amp; Roadmapping</h2>
    <p>Moving from discovery into architecture, I took ownership of conceptual prototyping and feature prioritization.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Iterative Usability Cycles:</strong> Directed our research team through 5 testing cycles across 50+ real-world retail scenarios, using tree testing to organize navigation and card sorts to structure menus.</li>
      <li><strong>Touchscreen Testing with Store Associates:</strong> Tested button densities, touch-target bounding boxes, and scan-to-tender flows with cashiers under simulated peak-hour cognitive load.</li>
      <li><strong>Enforcing a Strict Prioritization Matrix:</strong> With hundreds of feature requests from business and operations, I worked with Product Directors to balance user impact against engineering complexity, keeping the MVP focused on transactional stability and checkout speed.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- Concept Testing & Associate Feedback -->
    <img src="/images/pos/live_09.png" alt="Concept testing feedback: Guest Basket layout" class="project-image" loading="lazy" />
    <img src="/images/pos/live_10.png" alt="Basic Transaction positive feedback and design opportunities" class="project-image" loading="lazy" />
    <img src="/images/uxr/live_07.png" alt="Navigation tree test and card sorting structure" class="project-image" loading="lazy" />

    <h2>Phase 3: Execution Across Core Workstreams</h2>
    <p>When engineering began, work split into dedicated cross-functional pods. I led product design across two critical workstreams: Store Management and Loyalty Integration.</p>

    <!-- Colleague Core UI -->
    <img src="/images/pos/live_08.png" alt="Colleague Landing Page interface and expectations" class="project-image" loading="lazy" />

    <h3>Workstream 1: Store Management Workflows</h3>
    <p>Legacy manager tools were buried inside multi-level menus and often required switching between physical devices. I redesigned this into a centralized management hub with role-based permissions, allowing shift supervisors to approve register overrides and complete end-of-day balances in seconds.</p>

    <!-- Store Management Shift Delegation Workflow -->
    <img src="/images/pos/live_11.png" alt="Shift Delegation and Manager permissions workflow" class="project-image" loading="lazy" />

    <h3>Workstream 2: Loyalty &amp; Personalization Integration</h3>
    <p>Making CVS’s ExtraCare program easy to use at checkout was vital for business growth. The technical challenge was adapting legacy APIs into instant, responsive UI states so the register never lagged while scanning items.</p>

    <!-- Loyalty Architecture: Logic, Flows & APIs -->
    <div class="image-grid-2">
      <img src="/images/pos/live_12.png" alt="POS & Loyalty: Logic and Flows" loading="lazy" />
      <img src="/images/pos/live_13.png" alt="POS & Loyalty: API's and CRM Integration" loading="lazy" />
    </div>

    <p>Key initiatives shipped within this pod:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Customer-Facing ExtraCare Enrollment:</strong> Replaced associate-entered registration with a clear, self-guided prompt directly on the payment pin-pad.</li>
      <li><strong>In-Lane ExtraCare Plus Upgrade:</strong> Added a 1-tap paid subscription upgrade directly as a register SKU, eliminating the need to cancel transactions mid-flight.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- Initiative 1: Customer-Facing ExtraCare Enrollment -->
    <div class="image-grid-2">
      <img src="/images/pos/live_16.png" alt="Enrollment Process: Select Program (ExtraCare Free vs ExtraCare+)" loading="lazy" />
      <img src="/images/pos/live_17.png" alt="Enrollment Process: Personal Details verification" loading="lazy" />
    </div>

    <!-- Initiative 2: ExtraCare Plus In-Lane Upgrade SKU -->
    <div class="image-grid-2">
      <img src="/images/pos/live_14.png" alt="In-lane ExtraCare Plus upgrade prompt" loading="lazy" />
      <img src="/images/pos/live_15.png" alt="ExtraCare Plus checkout SKU and payment" loading="lazy" />
    </div>

    <h2>Phase 4: Field Validation &amp; Nationwide Rollout</h2>
    <p>Before deploying software across thousands of retail locations, our team ran evaluative testing that mimicked real store chaos: barcode scan failures, dropped network connections, customer questions, and store noise.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Realistic Usability Testing:</strong> Tested real cashiers of varying technical fluency, measuring time-to-first-transaction, error recovery speed, and physical comfort.</li>
      <li><strong>Measuring Usability (SUS):</strong> We tracked the System Usability Scale (SUS) as our north star. Through data-driven design adjustments, we drove the platform’s usability score up to a <strong>"Grade A" 88 points</strong>, beating retail industry standards by over 10 points.</li>
      <li><strong>Delivering $300M+ in Operational Savings:</strong> By shaving seconds off transaction times across millions of daily in-store purchases, reducing associate onboarding time, and eliminating supervisor override bottlenecks, the redesign unlocked over $300M in verified operational savings.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- POS MOD SUS Usability Score Metrics -->
    <img src="/images/uxr/live_08.png" alt="POS MOD SUS Usability Score (Grade A 88 Points)" class="project-image" loading="lazy" />

    <h2>Leadership Retrospective</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Field Research De-Risks Expensive Engineering:</strong> Watching real associates in messy store environments caught dozens of edge cases early, saving our engineering teams from costly post-launch rewrites.</li>
      <li><strong>Software Ergonomics Depend on Physical Context:</strong> At the counter, usability is directly tied to hardware angles, ambient lighting, and counter height. Good design leadership requires testing software in the physical world where it actually lives.</li>
      <li><strong>Empower Researchers to Speak Directly to Product Leaders:</strong> When researchers present their own field insights directly to product and business directors, design transitions from an execution team into a core strategic partner.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
